Paragraph  vice-president of the United States and a former U.S. Senator from Illinois. He was elected president in November 2008, defeating Republican nominee John McCain with 53% of the popular vote and 68% of electoral votes. Obama is the first African American to be elected as President of the United States.

Obama's presidency began on January 20, 2009. During his tenure, he signed into law economic stimulus packages in response to the Great Recession; reformed healthcare with the Affordable Care Act (commonly known as "Obamacare"); and ended U.S. military involvement in Iraq by December 2011 while increasing troop levels in Afghanistan. He also ordered targeted killings of terrorists, including Osama bin Laden, leader of al-Qaeda, on May 2, 2011.

Obama was reelected president in November 2012, defeating Republican nominee Mitt Romney with 51% of the popular vote and 62% of electoral votes. During his second term, Obama's administration negotiated a nuclear deal with Iran, signed the Paris Agreement on climate change, and promoted gun control in response to several mass shootings.

Obama left office on January 20, 2017, after serving two terms as president. He is currently residing in Washington D.C., where he continues his work through various initiatives such as the Obama Foundation.
